WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:03.990
Hey guys!

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.910
This is Antoine Allen, award-winning filmmaker.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.720
And my name is Lester Greene and I’m a producer,
actor, and published author.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.719
This is Film Courage, diary series.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:15.509
[Cultivating Success and Building Your Brand
in the Film Industry]

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:19.770
Lester: When I first started auditioning for
student films and short films and I met a

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.910
director by the name of Rickey Waymer and
he gave me my first role.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.830
And ever since then I just fell in love with
acting.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.199
I just love the idea of being in front of
the camera and being a different character,

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:31.940
that’s how I started.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.800
Antoine: I started actually in the music business.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.520
I used to manage up-and-coming talent and
I also used to do music videos.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.890
And from there it was a great fill-in but
I felt limited.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.190
So I wrote my first short film called SPLIT
/ DECISION and I won my first award and that

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.170
was it, that was like the love, love at first
sight.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.170
[How we began collaborating]

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.460
Lester: Antione has a feature film called
LIFE IS TOO SHORT and Crystal Williams introduced

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.420
me to him and told me about the casting call
that he had so Antione looked at my reel and

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.890
he saw one thing that he liked.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.890
Antoine: Just one.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.450
Lester: It was just one thing and he said
“Yeah, I want this guy in my movie.”

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.950
And so ever since then we’ve just been knocking
out films.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:16.592
Antoine: Yeah…I cast Lester because as a
filmmaker and as a writer you want to get

00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:21.320
the top-notch talent you can on our level
to make your stories come to life.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:26.719
So he was in my award-winning feature film
LIFE IS TOO SHORT and his role was really

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.230
powerful and from there I started doing more
films.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.240
I always remembered him.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.189
I said “Lester, you could do so much more.”

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:36.679
And sometimes it takes someone to see something
in you that you don’t see in yourself.

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:40.229
And from there we’ve just been knocking
out short films back to back to fulfill our

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:41.229
brand.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:45.060
Lester: Yeah, he was just telling me that
it’s so important to create your own work.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.210
Don’t wait for someone to cast you in a
film or someone to put you in a web series.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.049
And so we just created…what was that…it
was the first short film that we did?

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:57.249
Antoine: So far it was like five short films
together.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:59.780
And a lot of people say “Short films are
dead.”

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.999
It depends who you ask.

00:02:01.999 --> 00:02:04.709
So four to five short films.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:09.100
And what I did was I used those short films
to build the Antoine Allen brand and we’re

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.209
all part of the same brand.

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:11.209
[Build Your Brand]

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:15.420
And each short film won awards, we got short
films on TV.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.720
Lester: CBS.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.020
Antoine: CBS.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.690
We had short films show nationally and we
won national awards.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.740
And what that does is that builds your brand
and builds your calling card.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:29.620
For me I wanted to give back to the film community
in a way that it felt like it would be effective.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.632
So I started my own film festival called Validate
Yourself Film Festival.

00:02:32.632 --> 00:02:39.340
And the reason I call it validate is because
I think for a lot of artists they look for

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.040
validation everywhere else except themselves.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.870
I went to a few film festivals when I was
traveling the film festival circuit and some

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.290
festivals I just didn’t like how they were
treating filmmakers.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:54.340
Some times it felt like the filmmaker should
be honored to even be at the festival.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.980
A film festival and the filmmaker is partnership.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.040
You can’t have a film festival without having
filmmakers and a filmmaker needs a festival,

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.470
so it’s a partnership.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:03.550
It’s not lopsided.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:09.230
I came from the music business and I realized
that if you’re a hiphop artist, mixtapes

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.450
or demos are kind of like your calling card.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.890
So the short film is kind of like your calling
card.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.150
You do enough of it, you flood the market.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:22.140
I tell people all the time “You have to
grind like an indie, but move like a major.”

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.140
Lester: Ooohhh, say it again.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.320
Antoine: Grind like an indie, move like a
major.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.360
Lester: …And move like a major…ooohhh,
that’s nice.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.750
Antoine: And what that means is as an indie
you do whatever you have to do.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.620
Anything that’s possible, anything that
serves you’ve got to do it.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.170
Move like a major is just because I have a
little bit of money, don’t mean my film

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.660
should look like an independent film.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:38.660
Lester: Right.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.760
Antoine: Do what you can to make your film
look bigger than what it is.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.520
And like I said, working together and find
your partner.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.720
Find someone that you can really connect with.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.720
[Tell A Good Story]

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.010
Lester: A lot of people don’t have a good
story.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.140
I learned that from this guy.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.110
It’s so important to have a good story because
you’ll watch films and it will look pretty.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:04:00.640
It will look great on the screen and everything
but there is no story, there is no substance,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.260
nobody is reacting to it.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.830
And we just finished shooting a short film
called RAW FOOTAGE, right?

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.070
And it screened at The Schomburg Center in
Harlem [New York] through the Omari Hardwick

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.650
Real to Reel Tour right.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.580
And people were watching our short film and
they were feeling it, they were laughing,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.519
they were joking around.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:21.859
And then there was another film that aired
right…

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:22.859
Antoine: Crickets!

00:04:22.859 --> 00:04:23.859
Lester: Yeah…crickets!

00:04:23.859 --> 00:04:25.330
No reaction, no audience reaction.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:26.330
And it looked great.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:30.340
Antoine: And honestly the film that was getting
crickets looked way better than our's.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.340
Lester: It did.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.340
It looked better.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.340
[Your Film’s Success Depends On The Audience]

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.340
Antoine: Better writing, better camera.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.340
But…there was no engagement with the audience.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.180
So at the end of the day, your film’s success
depends on the audience.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.180
Lester: Right.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.180
Absolutely.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:46.349
Antoine: Not the best camera, not the best
colorist because that doesn’t sell tickets,

00:04:46.349 --> 00:04:47.370
the audience member does.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.370
Lester: Right.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.870
Let me give you another example we screened
our film MAMMA’S BOY at Necessary Studios,

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.930
right?

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:59.030
And we’re watching the film and I’m watching
the film for the first time and I was crying.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.010
And Antoine was telling me that people in
the audience were crying, too.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.949
It was amazing and that very same film is
going to be screening where?

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:08.949
Antoine: At the African-American Academy in
Africa.

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:10.330
Lester: In Africa!

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.330
Africa guys.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:12.330
That’s an incredible festival.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:16.509
I didn’t even know anything about it, Antoine
told me it’s like the Oscars in Africa.

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:20.523
Antoine: So we did a story in Queens [New
York] that traveled all the way to Africa.

00:05:20.523 --> 00:05:22.460
It touched someone all the way in Africa.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.460
So you just never know.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.819
[Use Your Short Films To Market Yourself]

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:29.699
This video is really for you to use your short
films as marketing tools.

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:35.160
There are only so many festivals you can submit
to, so do things outside of a festival.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.919
Do your own screenings, do your own premieres.

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:42.780
There are a few websites out there that you
can submit your films to try and get on TV.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.430
One of them is shorts.tv.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:49.990
Another one for more African-American filmmakers
is AfricanAmericanShortFilms.com.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.479
There are a few other ones out there.

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:53.479
[Get Over Fear]

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:56.220
And there are so many different outlets to
show your short films.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:02.729
My journey of how I accomplished so much,
first is I had to get over fear.

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:08.780
Fear is one of the main reasons why most people
are living a life they are not happy about.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.490
Once I got over that I had to realize this
is my own voice and have faith in myself.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.740
God have me amazing talent and I don’t want
to waste it.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.819
Right now I am 37 years old.

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:24.699
I officially got into the film business (without
stopping, without pausing, without tippy-toeing)

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:26.289
at 31.

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:31.639
Right now I am at 37 and so far I’ve got
one feature film under my belt that won 15

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:34.039
awards (domestically and internationally).

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:39.680
I never traveled outside of the U.S. but my
first feature film took me to Spain, France,

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.189
I went to Africa.

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:46.400
And also I was in a publication book that
is now iTunes for the top 100 indie filmmakers

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.400
in the world.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.699
I made number five on the list.

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:55.440
And the bigger accomplishment I think I had
was helping other people.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:01.550
When I saw Lester (when he first met me) he
was just trying to find his way.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.780
Now he’s an author, award-winning producer,
award-winning actor, and that’s my bigger

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:06.780
accomplishment.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:13.449
And all those pieces happened because I listened
to myself and God.

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:14.763
[Do Your Research And Continuously Build Your
Knowledge]

00:07:14.763 --> 00:07:18.620
And also to, research sites like Film Courage.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.849
Film Courage is another tool that I use to
get information.

00:07:21.849 --> 00:07:22.849
Lester: Oooohh…yeah.

00:07:22.849 --> 00:07:25.830
Antoine: So Film Courage, they don’t know
this but for years I’ve been watching a

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.059
lot of their videos.

00:07:27.059 --> 00:07:29.159
I’ve been taking pieces, here and there
and there.

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:33.150
And sites like that are the reason why I’m
in the position that I am.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.889
Lester: And I’ve learned so much from just
watching those Film Courage videos.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:41.039
So kudos to you guys for giving us an opportunity
to learn.

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:44.379
Antoine: And the funny part about it, I used
to watch those videos and be like “I want

00:07:44.379 --> 00:07:45.509
to be on Film Courage one day.

00:07:45.509 --> 00:07:48.080
I have courage.”

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.270
And look now…I’m on Film Courage.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.270
Lester: Right, right.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.620
Antoine: Behind-the-scenes diary, original
series.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:53.799
Lester and Antoine: Peace!

